<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Institute of Science</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as IISc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Institute of Science is a research university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1909. It was created with an endowment from the industrialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata">Jamsetji Tata</a>, with land and support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>. It has become one of India's leading institutions for scientific research and engineering.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea began with a chance meeting in 1893 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata">Jamsetji Tata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a> on a ship, where they discussed Tata's plan to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Five years later Tata wrote to Vivekananda of his scheme for a research institute of science for India. Interested in education and the skills needed for industrial projects such as steelmaking, he had sent his associate Burjorji Padshah on a world tour in 1896 to examine research institutions, and Padshah produced a guiding document, An Institute of Scientific Research for India, in 1898.</p><p>Tata formed a provisional committee, which presented a draft proposal to Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">George Curzon</a> on 31 December 1898. The Nobel laureate Sir William Ramsay was asked to propose a suitable place and suggested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The land and other facilities were donated by Maharaja Krishnaraja Wadiyar IV and by Tata. The maharaja gave about 371 acres (150 ha) and also granted Rs 500,000 towards capital expenditure and Rs 50,000 for annual expenses, while Tata gave several buildings. Nizam Osman Ali Khan also contributed around Rs 300,000 over 31 years. The vesting order that allowed the institute to function was signed on 27 May 1909. Early in 1911 the maharaja laid the foundation stone, and on 24 July the first students were admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of General and Applied Chemistry under Norman Rudolf and of Electro-Technology under Alfred Hay. The Department of Organic Chemistry opened within two months.</p><p>Morris Travers, a co-worker of Ramsay in the discovery of the noble gases, who had played a part in the founding, became the first director. The first Indian director was the Nobel laureate Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-v-raman">C. V. Raman</a>. The institute was the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to introduce master's programmes in engineering, and it was deemed a university by the University Grants Commission in 1958.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The main campus is fully residential and spreads over about 400 acres in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a>. Its Main Building, designed by C. F. Stevens and Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in 1912–13, is classical in style and fronted by a grey tower, and in front of it stands a monument by Gilbert Bayes in memory of J. N. Tata. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Departments</a> and centres belong either to the Science Faculty or to the Engineering Faculty. Research students make up more than 60 per cent of those on campus, and an undergraduate programme in science, begun during the centenary celebrations in 2009, admitted its first students in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian Institute of Science” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV (ruled 1894–1940, with full powers from 1902) and diwans such as M. Visvesvaraya, Mysore built the Shivanasamudra hydroelectric plant, the Krishna Raja Sagara dam, the University of Mysore and industries in Bangalore, which also hosted the <b>Indian Institute of Science</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
